The argument is worth giving in full. Suppose a one-loop diagram has sign $\sigma$, either plus or minus. Two disconnected identical loops have sign $\sigma^2=+1.$ Cut both loops at the same time. We now have two identical particles propagating between the cuts. Their ordinary two-particle wavefunction is symmetric for bosons and antisymmetric for fermions: $\begin{aligned}
\Psi_B(x_1,x_2)&=+\Psi_B(x_2,x_1),\\
\Psi_F(x_1,x_2)&=-\Psi_F(x_2,x_1).\end{aligned}$ The antisymmetry also explains the Pauli principle: if both fermions are put in the same state, interchange changes nothing physically but must reverse the wavefunction, forcing it to vanish.

